sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, single-swamp-node, DECISIONS R1 (record-only change starts no run), R2, R4, flow-tighten rule 22 (one docker command per step as exec argv; cfgmgmt docker_container stores its env as sensitive output the swamp role cannot write). Same shape as tool.remove.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: database:server:destroy
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: database:server:destroy<br/>app-mysql --force
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit database:server:destroy app-mysql --force`
  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the slug of the Database server as the argument
  %%> Confirmation:
  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No
  %%> - a JSON or non-interactive call without `--force` returns `database.confirmation_required`
  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing<br/>app-mysql · task #5160
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs app-mysql
  %%> - no Database connection points to app-mysql (`database.server_in_use`)
  %%> - no database create, user change or other task runs on app-mysql (`database.server_busy`)
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - takes the lock for app-mysql and marks it removing, so new databases on it are refused
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5160 recorded with the full intent
  alt the server has no Process
    Note over GW: Delete only the record<br/>R1: no run, nothing on a Node
    %%> When:
    %%> - a create failed before it recorded the Process
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Database server record is deleted; task #5160 succeeds at once
    GW-->>CLI: Server removed<br/>nothing on Node 12
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the result at once; there is nothing to follow
  else a Process runs it
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-server-destroy
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the Process and its container names, including the candidate and original names a create can leave
    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - credentials; removal needs no database login
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5160
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task number, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>@swamp/ssh · docker stop --time 60
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior, one command as argv
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - stops the server's container gently; one that is already gone counts as stopped
    SW->>N: Remove the container<br/>@swamp/ssh · docker rm
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - removes the stopped container; one that is already gone counts as removed
    %%> Not removed:
    %%> - the volume `orbit-app-mysql-data`, so the data survives
    SW->>N: Check it is gone<br/>@swamp/ssh · docker inspect
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - `docker container inspect` must report the container missing; that is the end check
    Note over SW: Report the result<br/>backend · failed_step · error_code
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - maps the first failed step to its error code, for example `database.server_remove_failed`
    Note over SW: Fail on an error<br/>assert · exit code 0
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - fails the run when the report holds an `error_code`, so the task fails with that code
    alt containers are gone
      N-->>SW: no container left
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the check passed for every container name
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
      %%> How the Gateway learns it:
      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
      Note over GW: Delete server + Process<br/>volume stays · task succeeded
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the Database server and Process records are deleted
      %%> - task #5160 succeeds
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - the volume `orbit-app-mysql-data` stays; remove it by hand when its data must go
      %%> - a new server with the same slug finds the old volume
    else a step fails
      N--xSW: exit ≠ 0
      %%> When:
      %%> - SSH fails, or Docker refuses to stop or remove a container
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      %%> The Gateway reads:
      %%> - which step failed and its error code
      Note over GW: Mark the server failed<br/>records kept · rerun after a fix
      %%> Result:
      %%> - Database server app-mysql and its Process stay recorded, marked failed
      %%> - task #5160 fails
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - containers removed before the failure stay removed; there is no rollback
      %%> - that is safe: a rerun inspects again and skips containers that are gone
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5160
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
    end
  end
